# A [[fish]] of the genus {{taxfmt|Scomber|genus}}.
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Last of the Scombers|journal=[[w:Fraser's Magazine|Fraser’s Magazine for Town and Country]]|volume=XLVII|issue=CCLXXIX|location=London|publisher=[[w:John William Parker|John W[illiam] Parker]] and Son,{{nb...|West Strand.}}|month=March|year=1853|pages=260–262|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_frasers-magazine_1853-03_47_279/page/260/mode/1up|passage={{...}} the familiar name by which all '''scombers''' are known at Montpelier being the ''peis d''’''avril'', or April fish. {{...}} All times and tongues have agreed to call the first of these '''scombers''' by some name allusive to the warlike weapon carried in his mouth—viz., a sword, several feet in length, finely attenuated in front, and, to the dismay of the denizens of the deep, of a temper like that of its owner, not to be trusted or trifled with. {{...}} The size and strength of these fish are as remarkable as their pugnacity; the power, as in most '''scombers''', residing in the muscles moving the tail.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=E[ugène] Pégot-Ogier|chapter_tlr=Frances Locock|chapter=General Scientific Observations|title=The Fortunate Isles; or, The Archipelago of the Canaries.{{nb...|By E. Pégot-Ogier. Translated from the French by Frances Locock. In Two Volumes.}}|volume=II|location=London|publisher=[[w:Richard Bentley (publisher)|Richard Bentley]] and Son,{{nb...|Publishers in Ordinary to Her Majesty.}}|year=1871|pages=230–231|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.ministry.02150/22272.93%2520A%252013/page/230/mode/1up|passage={{...}} large ''carongues'' and '''''scombers''''' are found in the Canarian seas, and the similarity of several species is most remarkable.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Charles F. Allen|title=Fish and Fishing: A School of Tuna off Navesink|journal=w:Sports Afield|volume=XLVII|issue=6|month=December|year=1911|page=525|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_sports-afield_1911-12_47_6/page/525/mode/1up|column=2|passage=All of the '''Scombers''' are of the most highly organized order of fishes, most. of them being without air-bladders, being so adapted to their native element that they have no need of them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Louis Roule|tlr=Conrad Elphinstone|title=Fishes and Their Ways of Life|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:W. W. Norton & Company|W. W. Norton & Company, Inc.]]|year=1935|pages=[https://archive.org/details/fishestheirwayso00roul/page/25/mode/1up 24–25], [https://archive.org/details/fishestheirwayso00roul/page/32/mode/1up 32], and [https://archive.org/details/fishestheirwayso00roul/page/232/mode/1up 232]|passage=The tail with its caudal fin, set vertically, is able to effect a rapid burst of speed which allows the fish to make a dash after its prey, but it cannot keep up this speed for long. At the rear it acts as a scull, beating the water first to right and then to left, and so pushing the body forward. Only some sharks and the large mackerel or '''scombers''' are capable of using this tool with an effect comparable to that attained by the cetaceans. {{...}} The whole construction of these large '''scombers''' is designed to make of them, like fast ships, the greyhounds of the sea. {{...}} The case of the tunny and the large '''scombers''', whose massive bodies become considerably warmer when they are swimming, is an exception.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Aleksandr I. Kuprin|Alexander Ivanovich Kuprin]]|chapter=The Læstrygonians|chapter_tlr=Bernard Guilbert Guerney|title=The Portable Russian Reader: A Collection Newly Translated from Classical and Present-Day Authors|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Viking Press|The Viking Press]]|year=1947|pages=[https://archive.org/details/portablerussianr00guer/page/n424/mode/1up 409], [https://archive.org/details/portablerussianr00guer/page/n427/mode/1up 412], and [https://archive.org/details/portablerussianr00guer/page/n440/mode/1up 425]|passage='''Scombers''' are being fried or marinated in every house. {{...}} Small '''scombers''' will pass through the wide mesh of the walls but become entangled in the inner meshes; {{...}} Kolya and I were drawing up a net we had put out for '''scomber''' the evening before, at right angles to the shore. The catch was a thoroughly poor one. About a hundred '''scomber''' were tangled in the meshes of the net, five or six ruff, a few dozen golden-hued, fat little crucians and a very great deal of jellied, nacreous medusae, looking like enormous, colorless mushroom-heads, each with a multitude of stems.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Tai-Sheng Chiu; Kwang-Zong Chang|title=Comparison of Ichthyoplankton Fauna in Northern Taiwan Strait During Winter and Spring|journal=Acta Zoologica Taiwanica|volume=5|issue=1|location=Taipei|publisher=w:National Taiwan University|year=1994|pages=23–32|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=kZQRAQAAIAAJ&q=scombers|passage=The abundant ichthyoplankton in spring are contributed from larval and early juvenile species of highly commercial fishery concerns, such as scads and '''scombers'''. {{...}} Scads and '''scombers''' are less abundant in the southern part of the Strait, relative to the northern Strait.}}

